   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.2. Increased capacity of the member States to apply international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNSDCF (2021-2025) of Turkey was drafted with joint efforts of UN Agencies including the ILO in 2020 and agreed by the Government in 2021 without its legal annexes. One of the Outcome of the UNSDCF aims to is to support the public institutions and the private sector in their contributions to more inclusive, sustainable and innovative industrial and agricultural development, and equal and decent work opportunities in collaboration with social partners, as well as to support people in attaining the necessary skills and capabilities to take an active part in and benefit from the transformation of businesses and the world of work.    

Priority to drafting of the UNSDCF, a Common Country Analysis was prepared in 2020 which has a strong emphasis on decent work, working conditions and international labour standards.

As a response to COVID-19, UN Agencies agreed to establish an inter-agency task force to regularly monitor and assess the socio-economic impact of COVID-19. The team drafted and published two reports namely &quot;UN Turkey Socio-Economic Impact Assessment Report&quot; and &quot;UN Turkey COVID-19 Socio-Economic Response Offer&quot; which were shared widely amongst the public institutions and the constituents.
 As being part of the UNSDCF Task Force established by UNCT in 2020 under the leadership of the UN Resident Coordinator, ILO played a critical role in integration of decent work and normative agenda of ILO into the cooperation framework. With the technical support and guidance of the ILO, a dedicated outcome on decent work and sustainable development was designed with a strong emphasis on cooperation with the constituents and prioritisation of actions specifically targeting vulnerable groups including women, youth, people with disabilities, informal workers, refugees and migrants in line with the principle of leaving no one behind. 
Further, ILO provided technical support and actively engaged in drafting of &quot;UN Turkey Socio-Economic Impact Assessment Report&quot; and &quot;UN Turkey COVID-19 Socio-Economic Response Offer&quot;. ILO was leading agency responsible for drafting Pillar 3 of the UN Turkey Socio-Economic Impact Assessment Report on Economic Response and Recovery in 2020. 
As part of the restructuring of the UN inter-agency joint action under the UNSDCF (2021-2025), ILO provided technical support to establishment of UN Result Groups (responsible for implementation and monitoring of UNSDCF) and development of Joint Work Plans of the Result Groups in 2021. Together with UNDP, ILO is co-chairing the Results Group on Inclusive and Sustainable Growth and Development, and Environmental Sustainability.</narrative>
   </description>
